            <ArticleTitle>Reproducibility of circulating endothelial cell enumeration and activation in children and adolescents.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>463-71</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2217/bmm-2015-0051</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">We examined the reproducibility of circulating endothelial cells (CEC) enumeration and activation among youth.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">CECs from 151 youth were measured at baseline and 1 week follow-up. Enumeration of CEC in fresh whole blood was determined by direct assessment of buffy coat smears (CD146+ nucleated cells) and activated CEC (%VCAM-1 expression) was determined after immunomagnetic enrichment and co-staining of nuclei, plus positivity for P1H12 and VCAM-1.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">No statistically significant difference in CEC enumeration (1.2 ± 2.5 vs 1.3 ± 2.2 CEC/milliliter of whole blood, p = 0.745) or activated CEC (57.1 ± 24.4 vs 58.0 ± 21.3 %VCAM-1, p = 0.592) between baseline and 1 week follow-up.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">On a cohort basis, CEC enumeration and activation are reproducible in youth. Relatively high individual biological variability may limit its clinical utility.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
